  "query_preview": "..(Tonight Through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1000 PM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nLatest water vapor imagery shows 500hpa ridge axis extending from  \nPacific NW southeast to the Gulf. Desert SW monsoonal moisture is \nstreaming through NV/ID then into MT and the Dakotas. One weak \nwave is located in southwest MT/northwest WY with another more \nrobust wave located in central NV. At the surface, Canadian high \npressure is nosing into the Dakotas with northeast to easterly \nwinds across our SD counties, and southeast winds across our WY \ncounties. At 17Z, temperatures across the CWA vary from around 70F\nacross the north, to the lower 80s across Campbell Co, WY and our\nsouthwestern SD counties. \n\nAforementioned wave located in southwest MT will slide into the  \nCWA this evening/overnight while surface high pressure ridge drops\nsoutheast becoming mainly located along the I-29 corridor of the \neastern Dakotas. Models continue to be bullish on pwat values, \nwith amounts running 150-175% of normal by 06Z Wednesday. The \nprobability of 1.50\" or greater pwat values across much of the \nCWA ranges from 50-90%, and for 1.75\" or greater ranges from \n30-40% basically across our southern counties. The probability of \nseeing 0.25\" of rainfall or greater from 06Z-12Z Wednesday is \n40-50% and stretches in a corridor from KGCC-KRAP-KICR. Will \ncontinue with scattered to likely pops in the overnight period \ntonight for much of the area. \n\nThere will be a brief dry spell from Wednesday morning through  \nearly Wednesday evening as subsidence behind exiting wave clears \nthings out. Pwat values still remain high, and by 00Z Thursday \nbegin to push 200% of normal. Stronger wave currently located in \ncentral NV is expected to impact the area around this time as \nwell, setting up the potential for some localized heavy rainfall \nto occur. Wouldn't be too terribly surprised Thursday morning to \nhear of some localized 1\"+ amounts of rainfall during the \novernight hours. \n\nUpper ridge then expands back into the mid-section of the country\nfor the weekend, with 850hpa temps climbing back to around +36C by\n00Z Sunday. Friday's highs may need to be trimmed down a skosh\ndepending on how much rain falls Wednesday night/Thursday morning\nand the resultant dwpts. We might need to evaporate some of that\nmoisture off before the mercury can really climb.",
